        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-37365",
        ":question/problem": "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{\u2218}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{\u2218}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{\u2218}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$"
      },
