        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-106934",
        ":question/problem": "In a restaurant kitchen, a pot of soup reaches a temperature of ${200}^{\u2218}\\text{F}.$ After turning off the stove, the temperature $T(t)$ of the soup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the kitchen temperature is ${76}^{\u2218}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the pot temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.",
